Consider the  $\{0, 120, 120, 240, 240\}$  set. Let the term with  $0$  degree angle on the complex plane be  $r$ . Then, we get  $P(x) = (x-r)(x-re^{2\pi i / 3})^2(x-re^{4\pi i / 3})^2$ . Nothing new here.

Now, consider the  $\{180, 300, 300, 60, 60\}$  set. Then, if we let  $r$  be the term with  $180$  degree angle on the complex plane,  $P(x)$  is the same thing because **relative** to the angle of  $r$ , say the  $300$  term has angle  $300 - 180 = 120$ , and the  $60$  term has angle  $60 - 180 = -120 = 240$  (mod 360), so the polynomial in terms of  $r$  is still  $P(x) = (x-r)(x-re^{2\pi i / 3})^2(x-re^{4\pi i / 3})^2$ .

