Let  $\sigma(n)$  denote the sum of the divisors of  $n$ . Then 
\begin{align*}
f(768) - f(384) = \frac{\sigma(768)}{768} - \frac{\sigma(384)}{384} &= \frac{(1+\dots+2^8)(1+3)}{768} - \frac{(1+\dots+2^7)(1+3)}{384} &= \frac{511 \cdot 4}{768} - \frac{255 \cdot 4}{384}  &= \frac{4}{768} = \boxed{\frac{1}{192}}.
\end{align*}

Really easy if you know the formula.  Just note that  $\sum_{p|n}d(n)=\prod_{i=1}^{k}\frac{p_i^{m_i+1}-1}{p_i-1}$ .  We see that 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{p|n}d(768)&=\left(\frac{2^9-1}{2-1}\right)\left(\frac{3^2-1}{3-1}\right)=511(4)=2044
\sum_{p|n}d(384)&=\left(\frac{2^8-1}{2-1}\right)\left(\frac{3^2-1}{3-1}\right)=255(4)=1020
\end{align*}
So our answer is  $\frac{2044}{768}-\frac{1020}{384}=\boxed{\frac{1}{192}}$  or choice  $\boxed{\textbf{(B)}}$ .

This is absolutely beautiful with generating functions. 

Consider the generating function of the number of singers   $$ \left(1+\frac 1x\right)^6(1+x)^8. $$   Excluding the "1" term, the sum of the coefficients of this polynomial that have  $x$  raised to a power of a multiple of 4 yield valid groups. The rest is computation with RoE filter: 
\begin{align*}
f(1) &= 2^6 \cdot 2^8 = 2^{14} 
f(-1) &= 0 
f(i) &= \left(1+\frac 1i\right)^6(1+i)^8 = (1-i)^6(1+i)^8 = (1+i)^2 \cdot 64 = 128i 
f(-i) &= \left(1+\frac 1{-i}\right)^6(1-i)^8 = (1-i)^8(1+i)^6 = -128i.
\end{align*}
Thus the answer is   $$ \frac{2^{14} + 128i - 128i}4 - 1 = \boxed{4095} $$   where we account for that extra zero-singer case by subtracting 1.

