We know the base and height of isosceles triangles  $\triangle PQT$  and  $\triangle RST$ , so we may find their areas with both the base-height formula and circumradius formula. After simplification we get the two equations
\[2+9a^2=6ar\text{ from }\triangle PQT,\qquad 3+4a^2=4ar\text{ from }\triangle RST.\]
This gives us a linear equation in variable  $a^2$ , and we solve to get  $a^2=5/6$ . We can use this to solve for  $r$  pretty easily, but this is not strictly required to compute the answer.

When we undo the affine transformation, the semiminor axis is still  $r$ , and the semimajor axis is  $r\sqrt{6/5}$ . This means that the ellipse constant (sum of distances from boundary point to foci) is  $2r\sqrt{6/5}$  (to see this, consider the boundary points at the ends of the major axis), which we can use to solve for the eccentricity in terms of  $r$  (take the boundary points at the ends of the minor axis). We can confirm that the distance between the foci is  $2r\sqrt{1/5}$ , giving an eccentricity of  $\sqrt{6}$ .</details>

