Order matters, so we want to evaluate
\[6\sum_{i=1}^\infty\sum_{j=1}^\infty\frac{1}{2^{i+(i+j)+(i+2j)}}\]
which means that the balls have been tossed into bins  $i$ ,  $i+j$ , and  $i+2j$ , in some order.
\begin{align*}
6\sum_{i=1}^\infty\sum_{j=1}^\infty\frac{1}{2^{i+(i+j)+(i+2j)}} &= 6\sum_{i=1}^\infty\sum_{j=1}^\infty\frac{1}{2^{3(i+j)}} 
&= 6\sum_{i=1}^\infty\sum_{j=1}^\infty\frac{1}{8^{i+j}} 
&= 6\left(\sum_{i=1}^\infty\frac{1}{8^i}\right)\left(\sum_{j=1}^\infty\frac{1}{8^j}\right) 
&= 6\cdot\frac17\cdot\frac17=\frac{6}{49}\end{align*}
giving  $\textbf{(A) }59$ .

