Expand with  $|z|^2 = z\overline z$ , such that 
\begin{align*}
12z\overline z &= 2(\overline z+2)(z+2) + (z^2+1)(\overline z^2 + 1)+31 
&=2z\overline z + 4z+4\overline z + z^2\overline z^2 + z^2+\overline z^2 + 40.
\end{align*}
What the heck is that 40 doing there? Oh wait, apparently it's  $6^2+2^2$ , okay.
\begin{align*}
z^2\overline z^2 - 12 z \overline z + 36 + z^2+\overline z^2 + 4 + 4z+4\overline z + 2 z \overline z &= 0 
(z\overline z-6)^2+(z+\overline z + 2)^2&=0.
\end{align*}
Thus  $\frac 6z = \overline z$ , so the answer is  $\boxed{-2}$ .**<span style="color:#9a00ff">Remark.</span>** This weird "completing the square" is mostly motivated by the fact that the answer extraction literally forces  $z \overline z = 6$ . Then, once one realizes  $40 = 6^2+2^2$ , it's all over.

Take the base 2 logarithm of both sides, obtaining
\begin{align*}
\log_2\left(x^{2^{\sqrt 2}}\right) &= \log_2\left(\sqrt 2^{2^x}\right) 
2^{\sqrt 2} \log_2 x &= 2^{x-1} 
\log_2 x &= 2^{x-1-\sqrt 2} 
\log_2(\log_2 x) &= x-1-\sqrt 2.
\end{align*}
The RHS is a straight line, which intersects the graph of  $\log_2(\log_2 x)$  at most twice. Obviously both solutions are positive. When  $x=2$ , the LHS is equal to 0, which is greater than the RHS; this indicates that there must exist a solution less than 2. When  $x=4$ , the LHS is equal to 1, while the RHS is greater than 1; this indicates that there must exist a solution between 2 and 4. Therefore   $$ 0+2 < S < 2+4 \implies 2 < S < 6, $$   which is equivalent to option  $\textbf{(D)}$ .

