We must have that  $-1 \leq \log_{20}(x) \leq 1$ , so  $\frac{1}{20} \leq x \leq 20$ . The period of  $\sin(20\pi x)$  is  $\frac{2\pi}{20\pi} = \frac{1}{10}$ , so in the range  $[0, 20]$ , the sine curve has  $200$  full periods of the form  $\left[\frac{k}{10}, \frac{k + 1}{10}\right]$  for  $0 \leq k < 200$ .

Note that in each period, there are two distinct intersections. This includes the final period, since at  $x = 20$ , the log curve is at  $1$ , while the sine curve is at  $0$  i.e. the situation is as follows:
[asy]
        import graph;

        size(10cm);

        real f(real x) { return 5 * log(x) / log(20); }
        real g(real x) { return 5 * sin(20*pi*x); }

        real xmin = 199/10;
        real xmax = 201/10;

        draw(graph(f, xmin, xmax, 2000), blue);
        draw(graph(g, xmin, xmax, 2000), red);

        clip(box((xmin, 5 * 0.99), (xmax, 5 * 1.01)));
[/asy]
However, one of the intersections (namely  $(1, 0)$ ) is a part of two different periods, and so it is double-counted, as shown below. 
[asy]
        import graph;

        size(8cm);

        real f(real x) { return log(x) / log(20); }
        real g(real x) { return sin(20*pi*x); }
        real h(real x) { return 0; }

        real xmin = 9/10;
        real xmax = 11/10;

        draw(graph(f, xmin, xmax, 2000), blue);
        draw(graph(g, xmin, xmax, 2000), red);
        draw(graph(h, xmin, xmax), black);

        label(" $(1,0)$ ", (1, 0), SE);
        dot((1,0));

        clip(box((xmin, -0.1), (xmax, 0.1)));
[/asy]
Hence, the total number of intersection points if  $2 \cdot 200 - 1 = 399$ , which corresponds to  $\boxed{{\textbf{(D)~399}}}$ .

