solution_md:
We claim the answer is  $(\text{D}) = 7$ .
We see that  $6$  is a pretty random number, which motivates us to generalize. We wish to find  $f(6)$  for
\[f(n) = \displaystyle\prod_{i=1}^{n}{(1+\frac{1}{i})}\]
Note that 
\begin{align*}
f(n) &= \displaystyle\prod_{i=1}^{n}{(1+\frac{1}{i})} 
&= \displaystyle\prod_{i=1}^{n}{\biggl(\displaystyle\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}{\frac{1}{i^j}}\biggr)} 
&= \displaystyle\sum_{0\leq a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, \cdots, a_{n}<\infty}^{\infty}{\biggl(\prod_{i=1}^{n}{\frac{1}{i^{a_{i}}}}\biggr)}
\end{align*}
We use the commonly known, snake oil method to get
\begin{align*}
\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\displaystyle\sum_{0\leq a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, \cdots, a_{n}<\infty}^{\infty}{\biggl(\prod_{i=1}^{n}{\frac{1}{i^{a_{i}}}}\biggr)x^{n}} &= \displaystyle\sum_{0\leq a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, \cdots, a_{n}<\infty}^{\infty}\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}{\biggl(\prod_{i=1}^{n}{\frac{1}{i^{a_{i}}}}\biggr)x^{n}} 
&= \displaystyle\prod_{i=1}^{n}{\biggl(\displaystyle\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}{\frac{1}{i^j}}\biggr)} 
&= \displaystyle\prod_{i=1}^{n}{(1+\frac{1}{i})} 
\end{align*}
From here, it is trivial that  $f(n) = n+1$ . This proof is left as an exercise to the reader. Thus, the answer is  $f(6) = 6+1 = \boxed{\frac{7}{2}} = (\text{C})$   $\blacksquare$

@above: I'll just go into a more in-depth solution.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>This is equal to the system of congruences:  $$ \left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
x \equiv 2 \text{ (mod 6)} 
x \equiv 5 \text{ (mod 9)} 
x \equiv 7 \text{ (mod 11)} 
\end{array}
\right. $$  Using the Chinese Remainder Theorem helps to give the general solutions to some modulo congruence equation. We can use this general equation and then substitute it into the next equation, find another general solution, and so on...

Start at the first one. We want to represent the general solutions to  $x \equiv 2 \text{ (mod 6)}$  using a general solution. We see that the first few positive solutions are  $2, 8, 14, 20 …$ , so  $x=2+6a$  where  $a$  is an integer. We now sub this in the second equation:  $x \equiv 5 \text{ (mod 9)} \rightarrow 6a+2 \equiv 5 \text{ (mod 9)} \rightarrow 6a \equiv 3 \text{ (mod 9)}$ . Finding the first few values of  $a$  that work, we get  $a=2, 5, 8 …$ , so to write a general form for this, we get  $a=2+3b$  for  $b$  is an integer. Subbing back into the first equation, we have  $x=2+6(2+3b) \rightarrow x=18b+14$ . Sub into the last mod equation. We have  $18b+14 \equiv 7 \text{ (mod 11)} \rightarrow 18b \equiv -7 \text{ (mod 11)} \rightarrow 18b \equiv 4 \text{ (mod 11)}$  because  $-7 \equiv 4 \text{ (mod 11)}$ . The first few solutions to this equation are  $b=10, 21, 32...$ , so  $b=10+11c$ , where  $c$  is an integer. Subbing back, we get  $x=18(10+11c)+14 \rightarrow x=198c+194$ . The three-digit numbers that work are  $194, 392, 590, 788$ , and  $986$ , thus the answer is  $\boxed{\textbf{(E) }5}$ .</details>

