**Problem #21:** A game board consists of 64 squares that alternate in color between black and white. The figure below shows square  $P$  in the bottom row and square  $Q$  in the top row. A marker is placed at  $P.$  A step consists of moving the marker onto one of the adjoining white squares in the row above. How many 7-step paths are there from  $P$  to  $Q?$  (The figure shows a sample path.)
[asy] size(200);
int[] x = {6, 5, 4, 5, 6, 5, 6};
int[] y = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7};
int N = 7;
for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
for (int j = 0; j < 8; ++j) {
draw((i,j)--(i+1,j)--(i+1,j+1)--(i,j+1)--(i,j));
if ((i+j) % 2 == 0) {
filldraw((i,j)--(i+1,j)--(i+1,j+1)--(i,j+1)--(i,j)--cycle,black);
}
}
}
for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
draw(circle((x[i],y[i])+(0.5,0.5),0.35),grey);
}
label(" $P$ ", (5.5, 0.5));
label(" $Q$ ", (6.5, 7.5)); [/asy] $\textbf{(A) }28 \qquad \textbf{(B) }30 \qquad \textbf{(C) }32 \qquad \textbf{(D) }33 \qquad \textbf{(E) }35$ <details><summary>Solution 1</summary>Notice that to travel from  $P$  to any particular white square, the marker must have come from one of the 1 or 2 squares immediately beneath it, since the marker can only travel to white squares. Then, the number of ways to travel from  $P$  to that square is the sum of the ways to travel to the two squares beneath it. We can then accordingly construct the following diagram:
[asy]
// Diagram by AoPS Wiki
int N = 7;
for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
for (int j = 0; j < 8; ++j) {
draw((i,j)--(i+1,j)--(i+1,j+1)--(i,j+1)--(i,j));
if ((i+j) % 2 == 0) {
filldraw((i,j)--(i+1,j)--(i+1,j+1)--(i,j+1)--(i,j)--cycle,black);
}
}
}
label(" $1$ ", (5.5, .5));
label(" $1$ ", (4.5, 1.5));
label(" $1$ ", (6.5, 1.5));
label(" $1$ ", (3.5, 2.5));
label(" $1$ ", (7.5, 2.5));
label(" $2$ ", (5.5, 2.5));
label(" $1$ ", (2.5, 3.5));
label(" $3$ ", (6.5, 3.5));
label(" $3$ ", (4.5, 3.5));
label(" $4$ ", (3.5, 4.5));
label(" $3$ ", (7.5, 4.5));
label(" $6$ ", (5.5, 4.5));
label(" $10$ ", (4.5, 5.5));
label(" $9$ ", (6.5, 5.5));
label(" $19$ ", (5.5, 6.5));
label(" $9$ ", (7.5, 6.5));
label(" $28$ ", (6.5, 7.5));
[/asy]
Our answer is thus  $\boxed{\textbf{(A) }28}.$</details> <details><summary>Solution 2</summary>Let us extend the chessboard with two additional columns to the right, as shown. Note that the blue line shows the right edge of the board.
[asy]
// Diagram by AoPS Wiki
int N = 7;
for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
for (int j = 0; j < 8; ++j) {
draw((i,j)--(i+1,j)--(i+1,j+1)--(i,j+1)--(i,j));
if ((i+j) % 2 == 0) {
filldraw((i,j)--(i+1,j)--(i+1,j+1)--(i,j+1)--(i,j)--cycle,black);
}
}
}
draw((8,0) -- (8,8),blue);
label(" $P$ ", (5.5,.5));
label(" $Q$ ", (6.5,7.5));
label(" $X$ ", (8.5,3.5));
label(" $Y$ ", (8.5,5.5));
[/asy]
The number of paths to travel from  $P$  to  $Q$  including the number of paths that cross the red line is clearly  $\tbinom{7}{3}=35,$  since the marker must travel three left-up and four right-up steps.

Now, proceeding with complementary counting, we wish to count the number of paths that travel through  $X$  or  $Y.$  There is exactly one way to travel from  $P$  to  $X;$  namely, three right-up steps, and from there, there are  $\tbinom{4}{3}=4$  ways to travel from  $X$  to  $Q,$  since the marker must travel three left-up and one right-up steps. Also, there are  $\tbinom{5}{1}=5$  ways to travel from  $P$  to  $Y,$  since the marker must travel one left-up and five right-up steps, and from there, there is exactly one way to travel from  $Y$  to  $Q;$  namely, two left-up steps. However, we must subtract the number of paths that travel through both  $X$  and  $Y,$  which is 2 - there is one way to go from  $P$  to  $X,$  two ways to go from  $X$  to  $Y,$  and one way to go from  $Y$  to  $Q.$  Thus, there are 4 + 5 - 2 = 7 ways to travel from  $P$  to  $Q$  that travel through  $X$  or  $Y.$ Finally, there are  $35-7=\boxed{\textbf{(A)}}~28$  ways to travel from  $P$  to  $Q$  that do not cross the red line.</details>

