From my [2020 AMC 8 Solutions](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/h2573512)

[font=futura]<span style="font-size:150%">**<span style="color:#58a1ad">16</span> Problem 16**</span>[/font]
[rule]
Each of the points  $A$ ,  $B$ ,  $C$ ,  $D$ ,  $E$ , and  $F$  in the figure below represents a different digit from  $1$  to  $6$ . Each of the five lines shown passes through some of these points. The digits along each line are added to produce five sums, one for each line. The total of the five sums is  $47$ . What is the digit represented by  $B$ ?

[asy]
import olympiad;
    size(200);
dotfactor = 10;

pair p1 = (-28,0);
pair p2 = (-111,213);
draw(p1--p2,linewidth(1));

pair p3 = (-160,0);
pair p4 = (-244,213);
draw(p3--p4,linewidth(1));

pair p5 = (-316,0);
pair p6 = (-67,213);
draw(p5--p6,linewidth(1));

pair p7 = (0, 68);
pair p8 = (-350,10);
draw(p7--p8,linewidth(1));

pair p9 = (0, 150);
pair p10 = (-350, 62);
draw(p9--p10,linewidth(1));

pair A = intersectionpoint(p1--p2, p5--p6);
dot(" $A$ ", A, 2*W);

pair B = intersectionpoint(p5--p6, p3--p4);
dot(" $B$ ", B, 2*WNW);

pair C = intersectionpoint(p7--p8, p5--p6);
dot(" $C$ ", C, 1.5*NW);

pair D = intersectionpoint(p3--p4, p7--p8);
dot(" $D$ ", D, 2*NNE);

pair EE = intersectionpoint(p1--p2, p7--p8);
dot(" $E$ ", EE, 2*NNE);

pair F = intersectionpoint(p1--p2, p9--p10);
dot(" $F$ ", F, 2*NNE);
[/asy] $\textbf{(A) }1\qquad \textbf{(B) }2\qquad \textbf{(C) }3\qquad \textbf{(D) }4\qquad \textbf{(E) }5$ [rule]
[font=futura]<span style="font-size:150%">**Solution**</span>[/font]
[rule]
[font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Answer (E):**</span>[/font] Finding the five sums, we get, from left to right,
\begin{align*}
    \text{first line} &= C+D+E
    \text{second line} &= B+F
    \text{third line} &= C+B+A
    \text{fourth line} &= B+D
    \text{fifth line} &= A+F+E.
\end{align*}
Note that each digit will appear twice in the total of the five sums, except for  $B$ , which will appear three times. Thus, our total is equal to   $$ 2A+3B+2C+2D+2E+2F, $$   and it is also equal to  $47$ . 

The key realization is that, since we wish to find  $B$ , we can regroup our total as   $$ 2A+3B+2C+2D+2E+2F=2(A+B+C+D+E+F)+B = 47. $$   We are given that  $A$ ,  $B$ ,  $C$ ,  $D$ ,  $E$ , and  $F$  each represent a different digit from  $1$  to  $6$ , so their sum is equal to   $$ 1+2+3+4+5+6=21, $$   regardless of which order they are in.

Thus, we have that  $A+B+C+D+E+F=21$  and  $2(A+B+C+D+E+F)+B = 47$ . Substituting gives  $2(21) + B = 47$ , so  $42+B=47$ , from which it is easy to see that  $B = \definecolor{maincolor}{rgb}{0.345098039, 0.631372549, 0.678431373}\color{maincolor}\boxed{\textbf{(E) }5}.$   $\definecolor{maincolor}{rgb}{0.345098039, 0.631372549, 0.678431373}\color{maincolor}\blacksquare$ [rule]

