[font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Answer (D):**</span>[/font] Note that the total population of all  $20$  cities is equal to  $20$  times the average population of all the cities. As well, on the left of the graph, we see that, since there are four smaller lines between  $4{,}000$  and  $6{,}000$ , each new smaller line represents an increase of  $500$  people. 

Thus, the average population of all the cities (represented by the horizontal dashed line) is slightly higher than  $4{,}000 + 1 \cdot 500 = 4{,}500$  and slightly lower than  $4{,}000 + 2 \cdot 500 = 5{,}000$ , so the total population is greater than   $$ 4{,}500 \cdot 20 = 90{,}000 \quad \text{and less than} \quad 5000 \cdot 20 = 100{,}000, $$   so the approximation of  $\definecolor{maincolor}{rgb}{0.345098039, 0.631372549, 0.678431373}\color{maincolor}\boxed{\textbf{(D) }95{,}000}$  people will always be the closest to the total population of all  $20$  cities.  $\definecolor{maincolor}{rgb}{0.345098039, 0.631372549, 0.678431373}\color{maincolor}\blacksquare$ [rule]

