[font=futura]<span style="font-size:150%">**<span style="color:#58a1ad">25</span> Problem 25**</span>[/font]
[rule]
Rectangles  $R_1$  and  $R_2$ , and squares  $S_1$ ,  $S_2$ , and  $S_3$ , shown below, combine to form a rectangle that is  $3322$  units wide and  $2020$  units high. What is the side length of  $S_2$  in units?

[asy]
size(200);
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,3)--(0,3)--(0,0));
draw((3,0)--(3,1)--(0,1));
draw((3,1)--(3,2)--(5,2));
draw((3,2)--(2,2)--(2,1)--(2,3));
label(" $R_1$ ",(3/2,1/2));
label(" $S_3$ ",(4,1));
label(" $S_2$ ",(5/2,3/2));
label(" $S_1$ ",(1,2));
label(" $R_2$ ",(7/2,5/2));
[/asy] $\textbf{(A) }651\qquad\textbf{(B) }655\qquad\textbf{(C) }656\qquad\textbf{(D) }662\qquad\textbf{(E) }666$ [rule]
[font=futura]<span style="font-size:150%">**Solution**</span>[/font]
[rule]
[font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Answer (A):**</span>[/font] Since there are more variables involved with the side lengths of rectangles, we are motivated to look at the squares in the diagram. Namely, let the length of  $S_1$  be  $a$ , let the length of  $S_2$  be  $b$ , and let the length of  $S_3$  be  $c$ . 

Looking at  $a$ ,  $b$ , and  $c$  along the width of the large rectangle, we get that  $a+b+c=3322$ . As well, looking at  $a$ ,  $b$ , and  $c$  along the height of the large rectangle, we see that (vertically) length  $b$  is equal to the overlap between lengths  $a$  and  $c$ , so subtracting  $b$  would give the height of the rectangle.

Thus, we have that
\begin{align*}
    a+b+c&=3322,
    a-b+c&=2020.
\end{align*}
Using symmetry, we add the two equations, which gives  $(a+b+c)+(a-b+c)=3322+2020$ , so  $2a+2c=5342$ . Dividing both sides of  $2a+2c=5342$  by  $2$  gives  $\dfrac{2a+2c}{2}=\dfrac{5342}{2}$ , so  $a+c=2671$ . Thus, since  $a+b+c=3322$ , we get that   $$ b = (a+b+c) - (a+c) = 3322 - 2671 = \definecolor{maincolor}{rgb}{0.345098039, 0.631372549, 0.678431373}\color{maincolor}\boxed{\textbf{(A) }651}. $$    $\definecolor{maincolor}{rgb}{0.345098039, 0.631372549, 0.678431373}\color{maincolor}\blacksquare$ [rule]

