**Problem #24:** A large square region is paved with  $n^2$  gray square tiles, each measuring  $s$  inches on a side. A border  $d$  inches wide surrounds each tile. The figure below shows the case for  $n=3.$  When  $n=24,$  the 576 gray tiles cover 64[verbatim]%[/verbatim] of the area of the large square region. What is the ratio  $\tfrac{d}{s}$  for this larger value of  $n?$ [asy]
draw((0,0)--(13,0)--(13,13)--(0,13)--cycle);
filldraw((1,1)--(4,1)--(4,4)--(1,4)--cycle, mediumgray);
filldraw((1,5)--(4,5)--(4,8)--(1,8)--cycle, mediumgray);
filldraw((1,9)--(4,9)--(4,12)--(1,12)--cycle, mediumgray);
filldraw((5,1)--(8,1)--(8,4)--(5,4)--cycle, mediumgray);
filldraw((5,5)--(8,5)--(8,8)--(5,8)--cycle, mediumgray);
filldraw((5,9)--(8,9)--(8,12)--(5,12)--cycle, mediumgray);
filldraw((9,1)--(12,1)--(12,4)--(9,4)--cycle, mediumgray);
filldraw((9,5)--(12,5)--(12,8)--(9,8)--cycle, mediumgray);
filldraw((9,9)--(12,9)--(12,12)--(9,12)--cycle, mediumgray);
[/asy] $\textbf{(A) }\tfrac{6}{25} \qquad \textbf{(B) }\tfrac{1}{4} \qquad \textbf{(C) }\tfrac{9}{25} \qquad \textbf{(D) }\tfrac{7}{16} \qquad \textbf{(E) }\tfrac{9}{16}$ <details><summary>Solution</summary>Since  $d$  is the length in inches of the border surrounding each tile and each of these gray square tiles measure  $s$  inches on a side, the whole square region measures  $25s+24d$  inches on a side. By the given equation, we have   $$ \frac{(24s)^2}{(25s+24d)^2}=\frac{64}{100} \implies \frac{24s}{25d+24s}=\frac{8}{10}=\frac{4}{5}. $$   Flipping this equation, we find that   $$ \frac{25d+24s}{24s}=\frac{25d}{24s}+1=\frac{5}{4} \implies \frac{25d}{24s}=\frac{1}{4}. $$   Multiplying both sides by  $\tfrac{24}{25}$  yields an answer of  $\boxed{\textbf{(A)}}~\tfrac{6}{25}.$</details>

