[font=futura]<span style="font-size:150%">**<span style="color:#58a1ad">22</span> Problem 22**</span>[/font]
[rule]
When a positive integer  $N$  is fed into a machine, the output is a number calculated according to the rule shown below.

[asy]
    size(300);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.8)+fontsize(13));
real r = 0.05;
draw((0.9,0)--(3.5,0),EndArrow(size=7));
filldraw((4,2.5)--(7,2.5)--(7,-2.5)--(4,-2.5)--cycle,gray(0.65));
fill(circle((5.5,1.25),0.8),white);
fill(circle((5.5,1.25),0.5),gray(0.65));
fill((4.3,-r)--(6.7,-r)--(6.7,-1-r)--(4.3,-1-r)--cycle,white);
fill((4.3,-1.25+r)--(6.7,-1.25+r)--(6.7,-2.25+r)--(4.3,-2.25+r)--cycle,white);
fill((4.6,-0.25-r)--(6.4,-0.25-r)--(6.4,-0.75-r)--(4.6,-0.75-r)--cycle,gray(0.65));
fill((4.6,-1.5+r)--(6.4,-1.5+r)--(6.4,-2+r)--(4.6,-2+r)--cycle,gray(0.65));
label(" $N$ ",(0.45,0));
draw((7.5,1.25)--(11.25,1.25),EndArrow(size=7));
draw((7.5,-1.25)--(11.25,-1.25),EndArrow(size=7));
label("if  $N$  is even",(9.25,1.25),N);
label("if  $N$  is odd",(9.25,-1.25),N);
label(" $\displaystyle\frac N2$ ",(12,1.25));
label(" $3N+1$ ",(12.6,-1.25));
[/asy]

For example, starting with an input of  $N=7$ , the machine will output  $3 \cdot 7 +1 = 22$ . Then if the output is repeatedly inserted into the machine five more times, the final output is  $26$ .  $$ 7 \to 22 \to 11 \to 34 \to 17 \to 52 \to 26 $$  When the same  $6$ -step process is applied to a different starting value of  $N$ , the final output is  $1$ . What is the sum of all such integers  $N?$   $$ N \to \rule{0.5cm}{0.15mm} \to \rule{0.5cm}{0.15mm} \to \rule{0.5cm}{0.15mm} \to \rule{0.5cm}{0.15mm} \to \rule{0.5cm}{0.15mm} \to 1 $$   $\textbf{(A) }73\qquad \textbf{(B) }74\qquad \textbf{(C) }75\qquad \textbf{(D) }82\qquad \textbf{(E) }83$ [rule]
[font=futura]<span style="font-size:150%">**Solution**</span>[/font]
[rule]
[font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Answer (E):**</span>[/font] Let our  $6$ -step process be   $$ N \to N_2 \to N_3 \to N_4 \to N_5 \to N_6 \to 1. $$  If  $N_6$  is odd, then  $3N_6+1=1$ , so  $N_6=0$ . However,  $0$  is not odd, causing a contradiction, so  $N_6$  must be even and equal to  $1 \cdot 2 = 2$ , which is indeed even.  $$ N \to N_2 \to N_3 \to N_4 \to N_5 \to 2 $$  If  $N_5$  is odd, then  $3N_5+1=2$ , so  $N_5=\tfrac13$ . However,  $\tfrac13$  is not odd, causing a contradiction, so  $N_5$  must be even and equal to  $2 \cdot 2 = 4$ , which is indeed even.  $$ N \to N_2 \to N_3 \to N_4 \to 4 $$  If  $N_4$  is odd, then  $3N_4+1=4$ , so  $N_4=1$ , which is indeed odd. If  $N_4$  is even, then  $N_4$  must be even and equal to  $4 \cdot 2 = 8$ , which is indeed even, so  $N_4$  can be equal to either  $1$  or  $8$ .  $$ N \to N_2 \to N_3 \to 1 \sim 8 $$  [font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Case 1.1:**</span>[/font] If  $N_4=1$  and  $N_3$  is odd, then  $3N_3+1=1$ , so  $N_6=0$ . However,  $0$  is not odd, causing a contradiction, so  $N_3$  must be even and equal to  $1 \cdot 2 = 2$ , which is indeed even. 

[font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Case 1.2:**</span>[/font] If  $N_4=8$  and  $N_3$  is odd, then  $3N_3+1=8$ , so  $N_3=\tfrac73$ . However,  $\tfrac73$  is not odd, causing a contradiction, so  $N_3$  must be even and equal to  $8 \cdot 2 = 16$ , which is indeed even.  $$ N \to N_2 \to 2 \sim 16 $$  [font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Case 2.1:**</span>[/font] If  $N_3=2$  and  $N_2$  is odd, then  $3N_2+1=2$ , so  $N_2=\tfrac13$ . However,  $\tfrac13$  is not odd, causing a contradiction, so  $N_2$  must be even and equal to  $2 \cdot 2 = 4$ , which is indeed even.

[font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Case 2.2:**</span>[/font] If  $N_3=16$  and  $N_2$  is odd, then  $3N_2+1=16$ , so  $N_2=5$ , which is indeed odd. If  $N_2$  is even, then  $N_2$  must be even and equal to  $16 \cdot 2 = 32$ , which is indeed even.

Thus,  $N_2$  can be equal to  $4$ ,  $5$ , or  $32$ .  $$ N \to 4 \sim 5 \sim 32 $$  [font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Case 3.1:**</span>[/font] If  $N_2=4$  and  $N$  is odd, then  $3N+1=4$ , so  $N=1$ , which is indeed odd. If  $N$  is even, then  $N$  must be even and equal to  $4 \cdot 2 = 8$ , which is indeed even.

[font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Case 3.2:**</span>[/font] If  $N_2=5$  and  $N$  is odd, then  $3N+1=5$ , so  $N=\tfrac43$ . However,  $\tfrac43$  is not odd, causing a contradiction, so  $N$  must be even and equal to  $5 \cdot 2 = 10$ , which is indeed even.

[font=futura]<span style="color:#58a1ad">**Case 3.3:**</span>[/font] If  $N_2=32$  and  $N$  is odd, then  $3N+1=32$ , so  $N=\tfrac{31}3$ . However,  $\tfrac{31}3$  is not odd, causing a contradiction, so  $N$  must be even and equal to  $32 \cdot 2 = 64$ , which is indeed even.

Thus, the sum of all such integers  $N$  is equal to  $1+8+10+64=\definecolor{maincolor}{rgb}{0.345098039, 0.631372549, 0.678431373}\color{maincolor}\boxed{\textbf{(E) }83}.$   $\definecolor{maincolor}{rgb}{0.345098039, 0.631372549, 0.678431373}\color{maincolor}\blacksquare$ [rule]

