solution_md:
Note that the t's are actually x's in the original Putnam problem statement. The following operations are allowed thanks to the restriction that  $f(x) > 0$ . We have that  $$ g(x) = \frac{\int_{0}^{x} tf(t)dt}{\int_{0}^{x} f(t)dt} \Leftrightarrow \int_{0}^{x} tf(t)dt = g(x)\int_{0}^{x} f(t)dt. $$  Differentiate both sides to get
\begin{align*}
xf(x) &= f(x)g(x) + g'(x)\int_{0}^{x} f(t)dt 
f(x) &= \frac{g'(x)}{x-g(x)} \int_{0}^{x} f(t)dt. \tag{ $\star$ }
\end{align*}
We divide both sides by  $\int_{0}^{x} f(t)dt$  and integrate:
\begin{align*}
\int\frac{f(x)}{\int_{0}^{x} f(t)dt}dx &= \int\frac{g'(x)}{x-g(x)}dx
\ln\int_{0}^{x} f(t)dt &= \int \Big(\frac{1}{x-g(x)} - \frac{1-g'(x)}{x-g(x)}\Big)dx + C
&= \int \frac{1}{x-g(x)} dx - \ln(x-g(x)) + C 
\int_{0}^{x} f(t)dt &= \frac{A}{x-g(x)}e^{\int \frac{1}{x-g(x)} dx}.
\end{align*}
Substitute back into  $(*)$  and we are done.
